bool need_bswap = alu64 || (to_le == is_big_endian);
if (need_bswap) { __mark_reg_unbounded(dst_reg); }
if (insn->imm == 16 || insn->imm == 32) coerce_reg_to_size(dst_reg, insn->imm / 8);
Reason:
The `scalar_byte_swap` function mutates the destination register’s value (via bounds reset or truncation) but does not reset `dst_reg->id`. This allows a scalar ID shared with another register (e.g., from a previous `r1 = r0` assignment) to persist. After a conditional jump on the swapped register, the verifier incorrectly propagates the learned bounds to the linked register, leading to false confidence and potential out-of-bounds accesses.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (need_bswap || insn->imm == 16 || insn->imm == 32)
	dst_reg->id = 0;
Reason:
Resetting `dst_reg->id` to 0 explicitly breaks any scalar ID ties made before the byte swap. This prevents the verifier from incorrectly propagating bounds from the byte‑swapped register to another register that previously shared the same scalar ID, eliminating the false confidence and potential out‑of‑bounds memory accesses.

